e we need exactly k sunny lines, but we can have any number of non-sunny lines. So the argument is flawed. However, maybe they also need to consider that we have only n lines total, and we need to allocate them to cover all columns. Let's examine more systematically.\n\nWe have n columns. For each column c, we need to cover h_c points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. Each non-sunny line can be vertical (covers all points in a column), horizontal (covers at most one point per row, but each column has at most one point per row, so horizontal lines can cover at most one point per column as well), or slope -1 (covers at most one point per column). So only vertical lines can cover multiple points in a column. So to cover a column with h_c > k, we need at least one vertical line for that column. Because we have only k sunny lines, each can cover at most one point in that column, so at most k points covered by sunny lines. The remaining h_c - k points must be covered by non-sunny lines. Since non-sunny lines can cover at most one point per column (except vertical lines), we need at least h_c - k non-sunny lines for that column, each covering one point. But we have only n - k non-sunny lines total. So we need to allocate them across columns.\n\nBut we can also use vertical lines to cover multiple points in a column, reducing the need for many non-sunny lines. So the minimal number of vertical lines needed is something like: for each column c, if h_c > k, we need at least one vertical line for that column. Actually we need at least one vertical line for each column where h_c > k, because otherwise we would need h_c - k non-sunny lines for that column, each covering at most one point, but we have only n - k non-sunny lines total, and we need to cover all columns. But we could allocate non-vertical non-sunny lines across columns, but each such line can cover at most one point per column, but can also cover points in multiple columns? Let's examine: a ho