ly detailed (e.g., the case |p+q|=1, they claim q≥2 else p=0 which would be horizontal line; they then bound number of t as floor(k/q)+1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). This is a bit quick but acceptable.\n\nAlso, the claim that only three lines achieve the bound is based on solving p+q=±2, but they didn't explicitly argue that p,q must be integers with gcd=1 and q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q. They gave the three possibilities. That's fine.\n\nThus the only issues are minor justification gaps.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON output with fields:\n\n- verdict: PASS (since answer correct, reasoning valid, only justification gaps)\n- confidence: high, maybe 0.97\n- answer_correctness: CORRECT (since answer matches expected result)\n- issues: list of issues (justification gaps). Provide description, location (quote from solution), severity (maybe 3 or 4). Provide type: JUSTIFICATION_GAP.\n\nWe need to list each issue. Possibly we can list two issues: one for the reduction step, one for the bound derivation or the claim about only three lines. But we can combine them into one issue if we want. The guidelines: \"List of all issues found in the solution (empty if none)\". So we should list each distinct justification gap.\n\nPotential issues:\n\n1. Reduction to vertical lines: The solution claims that replacing each non-sunny line with a vertical line covering the leftmost uncovered column preserves coverage, but does not provide a rigorous justification. This is a justification gap.\n\nLocation: \"Replace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line. ... Hence after the replacement we still cover every point of S_n and we have exactly the same number k of sunny lines.\"\n\nSeverity: maybe 4 (major missing scope, but not critical). So severity 4.\n\n2. Claim that only three lines can achieve the bound: The solution states \"Equation (2.1) with |p+q|=2 gives three possibilities... No other sunny line can contain m+1 points, because an